## Why it existed

`ref` and `key` were never real props — React consumed them. So this silently failed:

```jsx
// Before React 19
function Input(props) {
  return <input ref={props.ref} />;   // ❌ props.ref is undefined
}
```

```jsx
// The fix: ref arrived as a second argument
const Input = forwardRef(function Input(props, ref) {
  return <input ref={ref} {...props} />;
});

// Parent
const inputRef = useRef(null);
<Input ref={inputRef} />
inputRef.current.focus();
```

---

## React 19

```jsx
// ref is now an ordinary prop
function Input({ ref, ...props }) {
  return <input ref={ref} {...props} />;
}
```

No wrapper, no second argument, and the component's type is simpler. `forwardRef` still works — existing code does not break — but there is no reason to reach for it in new components.

---

## When you need a ref to pass through

| Case | Why |
|------|-----|
| Focusing an input from a parent | Form libraries, error handling |
| Measuring a component's DOM node | Tooltips, popovers |
| Scrolling an element into view | Anchor navigation |
| Integrating a library that wants a node | Charts, editors |
| Design-system components | Consumers expect `ref` to work like a DOM element |

That last one matters most: a `<Button>` in a design system that silently drops `ref` breaks every popover and tooltip library that needs to anchor to it.

---

## Narrowing what is exposed

Raw DOM access lets the parent do anything. `useImperativeHandle` restricts it to named methods:

```jsx
function FancyInput({ ref, ...props }) {
  const inner = useRef(null);

  useImperativeHandle(ref, () => ({
    focus: () => inner.current.focus(),
    clear: () => { inner.current.value = ''; },
  }), []);

  return <input ref={inner} {...props} />;
}
```

---

## Callback refs and React 19 cleanup

```jsx
// React 19 lets a callback ref return a cleanup function
<div ref={(node) => {
  const observer = new ResizeObserver(onResize);
  observer.observe(node);
  return () => observer.disconnect();
}} />
```

Previously the callback was invoked with `null` on unmount and you handled teardown by checking for it. The cleanup return is clearer and mirrors how effects work.

## The React position

The docs state it plainly: they have found no case where inheritance beats composition for reusing UI code. In thousands of components at Meta, none of them extend another component.

---

## Containment — the children pattern

```jsx
function Card({ children }) {
  return <div className="card">{children}</div>;
}

<Card>
  <h2>Title</h2>
  <p>Anything at all</p>
</Card>
```

`Card` does not need to know or care what it contains. Compare with an inheritance approach where a `BaseCard` class would have to define hooks for subclasses to override.

---

## Specialisation — configure, do not extend

```jsx
// ❌ Inheritance thinking
class ErrorDialog extends Dialog { … }

// ✅ Composition: a specific case built from the general one
function ErrorDialog({ message }) {
  return (
    <Dialog
      title="Something went wrong"
      icon={<AlertIcon />}
      tone="danger"
    >
      {message}
    </Dialog>
  );
}
```

`ErrorDialog` *uses* `Dialog`. It does not need access to its internals, so `Dialog` can be refactored freely.

---

## Multiple slots

```jsx
function SplitPane({ left, right }) {
  return (
    <div className="split">
      <div className="left">{left}</div>
      <div className="right">{right}</div>
    </div>
  );
}

<SplitPane left={<Contacts />} right={<Chat />} />
```

---

## Why inheritance fits UI badly

| Problem | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| Fragile base class | A change in the parent breaks subclasses in unpredictable ways |
| Single inheritance | Need behaviour from two parents? Stuck. |
| Tight coupling | Subclasses depend on internals, so nothing can be refactored |
| Wrong relationship | A card containing a button is has-a, not is-a |

A `<Modal>` containing a `<Form>` containing an `<Input>` is **containment**. Modelling that as an inheritance chain describes something that is not true.

---

## Sharing non-visual logic

Composition covers structure. For behaviour, extract a custom hook:

```jsx
// Both components share the logic without sharing a hierarchy
function useDisclosure() {
  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
  return { open, onOpen: () => setOpen(true), onClose: () => setOpen(false) };
}
```

> The clean summary: **composition for structure, custom hooks for behaviour, inheritance for neither.**

## The structure

```jsx
import { createPortal } from 'react-dom';

function Modal({ isOpen, onClose, title, children }) {
  const dialogRef = useRef(null);
  const previouslyFocused = useRef(null);
  const titleId = useId();

  // Remember what had focus, restore it on close
  useEffect(() => {
    if (!isOpen) return;
    previouslyFocused.current = document.activeElement;
    dialogRef.current?.focus();
    return () => previouslyFocused.current?.focus();
  }, [isOpen]);

  // Escape to close
  useEffect(() => {
    if (!isOpen) return;
    const onKey = (e) => e.key === 'Escape' && onClose();
    document.addEventListener('keydown', onKey);
    return () => document.removeEventListener('keydown', onKey);
  }, [isOpen, onClose]);

  // Stop the page behind from scrolling
  useEffect(() => {
    if (!isOpen) return;
    const prev = document.body.style.overflow;
    document.body.style.overflow = 'hidden';
    return () => { document.body.style.overflow = prev; };
  }, [isOpen]);

  if (!isOpen) return null;

  return createPortal(
    <div className="backdrop" onClick={onClose}>
      <div
        ref={dialogRef}
        role="dialog"
        aria-modal="true"
        aria-labelledby={titleId}
        tabIndex={-1}
        className="modal"
        onClick={(e) => e.stopPropagation()}
      >
        <h2 id={titleId}>{title}</h2>
        {children}
        <button onClick={onClose} aria-label="Close">×</button>
      </div>
    </div>,
    document.body
  );
}
```

---

## The checklist

| Requirement | Why |
|-------------|-----|
| Portal to `body` | Escapes `overflow: hidden` and z-index stacking contexts |
| `role="dialog"` + `aria-modal` | Announced correctly; background is ignored |
| `aria-labelledby` | The dialog has an accessible name |
| Focus moves in on open | Keyboard users start inside it |
| **Focus trapped inside** | Tab must not escape to the page behind |
| Focus restored on close | The user returns to where they were |
| Escape closes it | Expected behaviour |
| Backdrop click closes it | Expected — with `stopPropagation` inside |
| Body scroll locked | The page behind must not scroll |

---

## The hard part

**Focus trapping** is what the code above still lacks. A correct implementation finds all focusable descendants, and wraps Tab from the last to the first and Shift+Tab from the first to the last. It has to cope with elements appearing and disappearing while open, and `display: none` making things unfocusable.

This is why the right production answer is:

> "I would use Radix UI or React Aria. Focus management, scroll locking and inert backgrounds have too many edge cases to be worth reimplementing — and getting them wrong makes the component unusable with a keyboard."

Being able to *describe* the requirements while choosing not to hand-roll them is the strongest position in this question.

---

## The native alternative

`<dialog>` with `showModal()` gives you the top layer, focus trapping, Escape handling and an inert background from the browser. Support is now broad, and it removes most of the code above.

## The pattern

```jsx
function Chart({ data, theme }) {
  const containerRef = useRef(null);
  const chartRef = useRef(null);

  // Create once — never recreate on every prop change
  useEffect(() => {
    chartRef.current = new ChartLib(containerRef.current, { theme });
    return () => {
      chartRef.current.destroy();
      chartRef.current = null;
    };
  }, []);   // deliberately empty

  // Push updates through the library's own API
  useEffect(() => {
    chartRef.current?.setData(data);
  }, [data]);

  useEffect(() => {
    chartRef.current?.setTheme(theme);
  }, [theme]);

  // React renders this div and then never touches it again
  return <div ref={containerRef} />;
}
```

---

## The rules

**1. React must not manage the library's DOM.** The container div is empty as far as React is concerned. If React tried to render children into it while the library also mutated it, they would fight and you would get "failed to remove child" errors.

**2. Create in an effect, not during render.** Rendering must stay pure; instantiating a library is a side effect.

**3. Always destroy in cleanup.** Chart, map and editor libraries attach global listeners and observers. Skip the cleanup and every navigation leaks one.

**4. Update, do not recreate.** Putting `data` in the creation effect's dependencies destroys and rebuilds the instance on every change — losing zoom, scroll and selection state, and performing terribly.

---

## Handling resize

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const observer = new ResizeObserver(() => chartRef.current?.resize());
  observer.observe(containerRef.current);
  return () => observer.disconnect();
}, []);
```

Most libraries measure their container once at creation and need to be told when it changes.

---

## StrictMode surfaces the bugs

Development mounts, unmounts and mounts again. A library that is not destroyed properly ends up with two instances rendering into the same node — usually visible as a doubled or flickering chart. That is StrictMode doing its job.

---

## SSR

Libraries that touch `window` or `document` at import time crash on the server:

```jsx
// Next.js — load only in the browser
const Map = dynamic(() => import('./Map'), { ssr: false });
```

---

## What this question is testing

Whether you understand that React's declarative model has a defined boundary, and that refs plus effects are the sanctioned way to cross it. It comes up often because almost every real app integrates at least one imperative library.
